WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:21.370 --> 00:00:25.726
How a Shotgun Works
Remington 870 Pump-Action

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:31.267
Shotshells are loaded into the magazine
through the bottom of the receiver.

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:36.735
The compressed magazine spring
keeps tension on the shells.

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:43.902
Pressing the 'action bar lock'
allows the 'fore-end'&nbsp;to be pulled to the rear.

00:00:44.165 --> 00:00:47.723
As it comes back,&nbsp;a shell is fed into the receiver.

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:55.303
Pushing forward on the fore-end lifts the shell,
and the ‘bolt’ guides it into the chamber.

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:04.153
As the fore-end moves back, a cutout on the
bottom of the slide engages the 'carrier dog'.

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:08.855
As the slide moves forward,
downward force is put on the 'carrier',

00:01:08.855 --> 00:01:11.403
acting as a lever to lift the shell up.

00:01:13.399 --> 00:01:16.800
The left and right 'latches'
allow shells into the receiver

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:19.680
under the control of the fore-end tube assembly.

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:25.577
Notches on the assembly move the
latches in and out at specific times.

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:31.563
The left latch holds the shell until
the fore-end is almost fully rearward.

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:37.495
After the first shell advances into the
receiver, the right latch catches the next shell.

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:42.751
As the fore-end is pushed forward,
the first shell is moved into the chamber

00:01:42.751 --> 00:01:46.251
and the left latch assumes control of the next shell.

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:50.720
When the bolt makes contact with the 'breech face',

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:54.787
the 'slide' pushes up on the 'locking&nbsp;block',
locking it into the barrel.

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:03.039
With the 'safety' in the off position,
the trigger is free to move.

00:02:05.192 --> 00:02:09.368
Pulling the trigger rotates the
'sear', which releases the 'hammer'.

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:15.895
The hammer strikes the 'firing pin',
causing it to travel forward and impact the shell primer.

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:25.499
The inside of a shotshell contains a primer,
powder charge, a wad, and shot, or a slug.

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:32.410
Inside the primer is a primer cup,
priming compound, and an anvil.

00:02:33.318 --> 00:02:35.916
As these are struck by the firing pin,

00:02:35.916 --> 00:02:39.716
a chain reaction&nbsp;ignites the powder charge
inside the shell,

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:42.719
and the wad is propelled down the barrel.

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:49.840
Just after leaving the barrel, the
wad opens so the shot can spread.

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:55.360
The 'extractor' grips the rim
of the spent shell case.

00:02:56.160 --> 00:03:00.080
As the fore-end is pulled to
the rear, the bolt is unlocked,

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:02.731
and the case is extracted from the chamber.

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:09.175
The bolt continues to the rear and the 'ejector'
kicks out the empty case from the receiver.

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:16.318
As the bolt comes back, the 'hammer'
is reset, and held back by the 'sear'.

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:22.255
When the fore-end is fully forward, the
'action bar lock' lowers the 'connector'.

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:27.847
Again, allowing control of the sear
with a pull of the trigger.

